				<description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This post is a continuation on &lt;a href=&#34;https://www.andreffs.com/blog/setup-n8n-on-kubernetes&#34;&gt;&amp;ldquo;Setup n8n on Kubernetes&amp;rdquo;&lt;/a&gt;, so most of the things we talk here assume that you&amp;rsquo;ve followed/read this one as well.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;You have your own n8n instance running in Kubernetes, taking advantage of all amazing functionality and capabilities of it, but you are still going in blind right now. I mean, how can you check if your service is healthy? Is it running? How much memory is it using? 🤔&lt;/p&gt;</description>
